Subject: the gun is nearer than every world pixel it borders (D-061)

the user, on 0.0.0.11: "my only complaint is how the GUN intersects or overlaps
with elements in the rendered world. it's a little disorienting, especially when
you get close to a wall and your eyes focus on the edges of your gun and how it
overlaps with the world around you." And, on being asked whether it was only
walls: "it's a little disorienting even when it overlaps with the ground. it's
disorienting often."

THE MECHANISM, verified before it was designed against.

  * PD clears the depth buffer before bgunRender (player.c:6120), so the
    viewmodel PAINTS OVER the world unconditionally. Occlusion says "the gun is
    in front" on every pixel of the gun's silhouette.
  * After D-060 the gun converges at its NEAREST vertex, so the whole weapon
    sits at the panel or behind it: measured, six matched blocks in the gun's
    own box, every one at -6 px and not one positive (M-045 addendum).
  * The world converges at Crosshair Distance. Everything NEARER than that
    carries crossed disparity, growing as 2*a*e*(1/d - 1/C) without bound. At
    the 0.0.0.12 defaults a wall 30 units from Joanna's face is 402 px on a
    2560 px eye - twelve degrees, far past fusion - and the floor she is
    standing next to, two metres away, is already 33 px in front of the panel.

So on every frame with the weapon up, occlusion and stereo said opposite things
about the same edge, and the size of the disagreement grew as she approached
anything. That is the whole of the user's paragraph, and neither half of it is a
comfort preference: it is a contradiction in the picture.

WHAT THE SIBLINGS DID, and why this is not quite theirs. quake3e hit exactly
this ("the weapon already sits at ~26x crossed disparity ... far outside fusable
range") and shipped D-028 v2: the weapon pass rendered with zProj = r_znear, so
the weapon is at the panel and never in front of it - which is what D-060
already does here, and is not sufficient, because quake3e's convergence is
160 units (~1.6 m) where PD's is 762 (25 ft), so quake3e's WORLD barely comes
out of the panel and PD's comes out of it constantly. q2repro deferred the
viewmodel explicitly ("a v2 concern") and then left the panel mode for
hand-tracked VR, where the weapon has real world depth. The harbourmasters
playbook's nearest relative is the aiming profile (depth x0.3, convergence
bias x0.5 while a first-person camera is up) - a global softening, not an
ordering rule. Nobody clamps the world, and nobody had to: no sibling both
converges 25 ft out and lets the camera reach a wall.

THE LAW. Write U for |a*e/conv|, the NDC shift of a point at infinity - the
whole of the depth the scene has BEHIND the panel, and the honest unit for the
depth it may have in front of it.

  WORLD   1/d is softly saturated at (1+N)/conv with N = 2, so no world pixel is
          more than U*N in front of the panel, and - the saturation being
          asymptotic - none ever reaches it. The clamp plane is conv/3, which at
          the shipped defaults is 254 units (8.3 ft): 33 px, 1.04 degrees, a
          wall rendered at 1.8 m in front of a panel at 3.6 m. The knee starts
          at conv/2 and the curve is C1, so nothing in the picture carries a
          crease and a surface sliding through the plane does not change its
          rate of approach in one frame.
  GUN     a constant NDC shift - a rigid move along the view axis - putting the
          viewmodel's FARTHEST vertex at U*G with G = 2.5 > N. Its own internal
          spread (D-060: nearest vertex at the convergence, the rest behind it,
          total exactly U) rides on top, so every vertex lands between U*2.5
          and U*3.5.

  THE INVARIANT: the gun is nearer than every world pixel it borders. Paint
  order and stereo order are now the same order, for the floor as much as for
  the wall.

WHAT THIS COSTS, said plainly. The 2D layer is untouched, at zero disparity, so
the ammo HUD is no longer the nearest thing on screen - the gun is, by U*3.5,
about 1.7 degrees. That is the same class of conflict D-060 removed, moved from
the gun's whole silhouette (every frame, growing without bound) to one corner
of the HUD (bounded, and only where they overlap). It is a trade and it is
the user's to judge; the alternative - moving the 2D layer forward with the gun -
takes the crosshair off the convergence plane the "Crosshair Distance" row is
named after, which is a worse bargain. Q-029 carries it.

Everywhere but visionOS PD_VISION_STEREO is 0 and every line of this is
preprocessed away. At Stereo Depth 0 % `e` is zero, the fold returns before any
of it, and the two eyes stay bit-identical - which the gates run.

